00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Welcome to this course on Nvidia's NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:07.099
which will show you how to harden your agent for production.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.800
This course is built in partnership with Nvidia

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.500
and is taught by Brian McBrayer,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.698
who is Solutions Architect in Generative AI at Nvidia.

00:00:15.736 --> 00:00:18.335
One problem that many of us face is

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:21.835
how to take that demo that works maybe 60% of the time

00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:23.936
and turn into something reliable

00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:26.236
that you can safely deploy to users.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.971
Nvidia's open source NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:34.070
or NAT gives key tools for doing so.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.871
Specifically, NAT makes it easy for you to add observability

00:00:39.042 --> 00:00:41.843
to build and repeatedly run evals

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:45.143
and also deploy your agentic workflow.

00:00:46.042 --> 00:00:48.843
Maybe you've already built an agentic workflow

00:00:48.942 --> 00:00:52.843
in raw Python or using a framework like LangGraph or Crew AI.

00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:56.045
You can then integrate NAT to get observability

00:00:56.045 --> 00:00:59.045
to see what your agent workflow is doing.

00:00:59.646 --> 00:01:03.045
For example, if your agent occasionally calls the wrong tool,

00:01:03.654 --> 00:01:05.853
NAT will show you execution traces

00:01:06.054 --> 00:01:08.453
so you can actually see what's going on.

00:01:09.153 --> 00:01:11.653
In addition, NAT makes it easy for you to set up

00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:14.153
and run evals in a disciplined way.

00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:16.200
This is very helpful if you

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.353
want to run maybe a CI/CD process

00:01:18.754 --> 00:01:21.353
or if you just want to drive a systematic process

00:01:21.353 --> 00:01:23.853
for improving your agent's performance.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.685
In this course, you will learn NAT

00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:29.284
by building an agent to analyze climate data.

00:01:29.584 --> 00:01:32.185
You'll start by registering Python functions as tools

00:01:32.284 --> 00:01:33.984
to add more functionality to your agent.

00:01:34.584 --> 00:01:36.385
Agents reason in natural language,

00:01:36.584 --> 00:01:38.584
so without the right tools, it's hard to know

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:42.484
why they made certain decisions. You'll use OpenTelemetry tracing,

00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:46.084
visualized in Phoenix, to see exactly how your agent reasons,

00:01:46.385 --> 00:01:48.885
which tools it considers and what choices it makes.

00:01:49.472 --> 00:01:51.403
You'll also evaluate performance over time,

00:01:51.802 --> 00:01:53.552
so when you adjust a prompt or change

00:01:53.552 --> 00:01:55.302
a model, you'll know whether it actually helped.

00:01:55.903 --> 00:01:58.602
NAT works directly with frameworks you already use,

00:01:58.802 --> 00:02:01.403
LangChain, Crew AI, or your own custom code.

00:02:01.926 --> 00:02:06.825
So, finally, you'll expand the climate analyzer agent to a multi-agent workflow

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:09.825
where specialized agents built with frameworks like LangGraph

00:02:10.126 --> 00:02:12.425
collaborate on different aspects of climate analysis.

00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:15.550
Deploying agents introduces so many complexities.

00:02:16.251 --> 00:02:19.050
You need an API endpoint, authentication, rate limiting.

00:02:19.651 --> 00:02:22.651
Suddenly you're writing infrastructure code instead of working on your agents.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.750
And all of these problems reappear anytime you want to iterate

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.550
on the models you're using, or the prompts you're passing in.

00:02:29.151 --> 00:02:31.503
Through several labs in the course, you will

00:02:31.503 --> 00:02:34.151
learn how NAT makes AI agents ready for production.

00:02:34.576 --> 00:02:36.977
Many people have worked to create this course.

00:02:36.977 --> 00:02:38.977
I'd like to thank from NVIDIA,

00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:43.176
Josh Wyatt, Michael Demoret, and Bartley Richardson.

00:02:43.176 --> 00:02:48.676
And from DeepLearning.AI, Summer Rae, Esmaeil Gargari, and Brendan Brown.

00:02:49.076 --> 00:02:52.977
In the first lesson, you build your first agent in NAT,

00:02:52.977 --> 00:02:54.777
connect it to an API endpoint

00:02:54.777 --> 00:02:56.877
and see it working in real time.

00:02:56.877 --> 00:02:59.477
Let's go on to the next video to get started.
